=== DEMO 1 STYLE: 5 clients, 5 different commands, all at once (3 workers, sync ON) ===
========================================================
 Server Started with 3 Workers
 Synchronization Mode: ENABLED (Mutex)
========================================================
[#1] [Worker-1] received LIST from Client-1
[#2] [Worker-2] received RESERVE 5 from Client-2
[#3] [Worker-2] entering critical section
[#4] [Worker-2] check Resource 5: AVAILABLE
[#5] [Worker-3] received RESERVE 8 from Client-4
[#6] [Worker-1] received STATUS 5 from Client-3
[#7] [Worker-2] Resource 5 reserved by Client-2
[#8] [Worker-2] leaving critical section
[#9] [Worker-3] entering critical section
[#10] [Worker-3] check Resource 8: AVAILABLE
[#11] [Worker-2] received CANCEL 8 from Client-5
[#12] [Worker-3] Resource 8 reserved by Client-4
[#13] [Worker-3] leaving critical section
[#14] [Worker-2] entering critical section
[#15] [Worker-2] Resource 8 cancel failed
[#16] [Worker-2] leaving critical section

[Server] Cleaning up Message Queue...

=== CLIENT RESULTS ===
Client-1 (LIST)      -> [1: A] [2: A] ... [20: A]  (snapshot taken before any RESERVE completed)
Client-2 (RESERVE 5) -> SUCCESS
Client-3 (STATUS 5)  -> RESERVED by Client-2   (correct: STATUS now also takes table_mutex, so it
                                                 never reads Resource 5 mid-write)
Client-4 (RESERVE 8) -> SUCCESS
Client-5 (CANCEL 8)  -> FAILED                 (correct: Client-5 does not own Resource 8, so the
                                                 ownership check in handle_cancel rejects it)

Demonstrates Demo 1's requirement (multiple clients sending different
commands at the same time) with no crash and every result consistent
with the actual final state of the resource table.
